.Dd August 19, 2026
.Dt T150D 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm t150d
.Nd force feedback daemon for the Thrustmaster T150
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl a Ar force
.Op Fl e Ar endpoint
.Op Fl g Ar ms
.Op Fl E
.Op Fl r Ar degrees
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl w
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the macOS half of the force feedback bridge. It listens on loopback for
the proxy
.Pa dinput8.dll
running inside a CrossOver bottle, turns the effects that arrive into the
wheel's own packets, and hands them to a backend.
.Pp
It needs no entitlement, no root and no system extension. It must run as the
console user, because
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
is gated on that privilege, and it must not be sandboxed, because App Sandbox
breaks IOKit HID access.
.Pp
When it takes the wheel it stops every effect slot and closes the wheel's
input, before anything else touches it. Effects and the input state both
outlive the process that set them, so a wheel inherited from a crashed daemon,
or from a probe that never cleaned up after itself, can arrive still rendering
that process's last effect. The scrub means it goes quiet at startup rather
than at shutdown.
.Pp
Then it opens the input again and holds it for as long as it holds the wheel.
That is not tidiness: with the input shut the firmware rests both pedals at
maximum, so a game which enumerates the wheel before the proxy has connected
calibrates them fully pressed and reads every press backwards from then on.
.Pp
It also releases the wheel's own centring spring, unless
.Fl a
asked for one. The firmware runs that spring whenever no application holds the
input, and it is felt as a stiffness about a point that never returns the
wheel to centre. A game's own forces do the centring, and the spring only
fights them.
.Pp
One client is served at a time. A second connection may displace the first,
because the usual reason for one is a game that crashed and was restarted,
but only after it has proved the token and only after the first has been put
back into a safe state. Proving the token opens the wheel's input, because
the newcomer needs it held from the moment it takes over; everything else it
would say to the wheel, its device gain among it, waits until the effects it
is displacing have been stopped. A newcomer that does not authenticate within
two seconds, or that sends anything other than a token, is dropped without the
current client noticing: anything on the machine can reach loopback, and
displacing on the connection alone would let any local process end a game's
force feedback at will.
.Pp
One daemon at a time per endpoint. Starting a second one against the same
endpoint is refused rather than allowed to fight the first: acquiring the
wheel scrubs every slot and closes and reopens its input, so a second daemon
takes a running game's force feedback away, and it publishes its own port over
the first's and removes it again on the way out, leaving a daemon that is
running perfectly well with nothing able to find it. The login agent and the
menu bar item's own child are the two ways to arrive here and neither could
see the other. Running several on purpose still works: give each one its own
.Fl e .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a Ar force
Leave the wheel's own centring spring at this, 0 to 10000, every time the
daemon takes the wheel. Zero releases it and is the default, because a game's
own forces do the centring and the firmware's spring only fights them.
.Pp
A game that sends no force feedback at all wants the opposite, and has no way
to ask for it: DirectInput has no property for a wheel's centring spring, so
on Windows it lives in the vendor's control panel. This is where it lives
here, and the application offers it in its menu as
.Em Centring spring .
.Pp
It is applied on every acquire rather than once, because the wheel forgets it
when it is unplugged and the daemon is the only thing that notices, and it is
what every safe state leaves behind rather than a hard zero. Writing zero
there undid this option on the first client to go away, and nothing put it
back until the wheel was physically replugged, so the setting survived one
game at most.
.Pp
A client's own centring spring is separate and wins while it drives: a game
that sets one with
.Dv T150_OP_SET_AUTOCENTER
has that restored after a re-acquire, and the value here is what the wheel
returns to once the game has gone.
.It Fl g Ar ms
Pause this long after every packet sent to the wheel. Off by default. The
probe tools have always left 20 milliseconds between writes, on a comment
saying the wheel drops back to back packets, but nothing has ever tested
whether that is needed and the interrupt OUT route works with none. A floor
on the write rate would cap effect updates well below what force feedback
wants, so this exists to put the pause back if a real wheel asks for it.
.Pp
It is capped at 20 milliseconds, which is the pause it exists to be able to
restore: that is what
.Xr t150-probe 1
leaves between its writes, and a lower ceiling would leave this option unable
to answer its own question.
.Pp
It came down from 50 because a client's round trip cannot absorb that. The
proxy waits half the watchdog, 250 milliseconds, for a reply before it gives up
and drops the connection, and everything the daemon writes while a frame is in
hand counts against that: an emission pass runs before the socket is read, and
the frame itself can be a reset stopping every loaded slot. At 50 the budget is
five packets, which a game stopping five effects at once spends outright; at 20
it is twelve.
Past that the proxy hangs up on a daemon that is working perfectly and
reconnects into a displacement, so this is a setting to measure with rather
than one to leave on. Nothing bounds the case where the wheel has stopped
taking writes and every slot owes a stop, because the retries are then the
traffic; that is a wheel whose force feedback has already failed, and the
watchdog rather than this is what makes it safe.
.Pp
It composes with the emitter rather than replacing it: the emitter bounds how
often a pass runs and this bounds how long one takes, so a large gap lengthens
a pass and therefore the interval between watchdog evaluations.
.It Fl n
Drive nothing. Print every packet that would have gone to the wheel instead,
in the form
.Xr t150-probe 1
prints. This is the only behaviour on anything but macOS.
.It Fl e Ar endpoint
Where to publish the port and token, and what the single instance lock is
taken on: a second daemon given the same path is refused, and one given a
different path runs alongside. The default is
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support/t150ffb/endpoint ,
which the proxy reads through Wine's
.Pa Z:
mapping.
.It Fl E
Let an emission pass run ahead of its four millisecond floor when the writer
has no backlog. Off.
.Pp
It was the default in 0.2.2, 0.2.3 and 0.2.4, and went back to off because it
is the only change to how this daemon drives the wheel in about thirty
releases and force feedback began stopping mid session in the release that
introduced it, with the wheel found back at its boot identity. Nothing proves
the two are connected and the measurement to settle it has not been made, but
nobody has ever felt this help either, so the suspicion is not worth carrying
by default. See
.Sx THE EMITTER
and RESEARCH.md A51.
.It Fl r Ar degrees
Lock to lock, 270 to 1080, applied whenever a client takes the wheel and
again after the wheel has been re-acquired. Unset leaves the wheel's own
range alone.
.Pp
No game can ask for this. DirectInput has no property for wheel rotation, so
on Windows the number is set in the vendor's control panel and a game merely
assumes the wheel is already at the one in its settings. A game asking for
900 degrees therefore reaches nothing here, and its steering is scaled for a
range the wheel is not at.
.Pp
.Sy "The wheel's range and the game's rotation setting must be the same number" .
DirectInput has no property for wheel rotation, so a game cannot move a wheel
and simply assumes it is already at whatever its own setting says. The T150 is
1080 degrees and Assetto Corsa defaults to 900, so out of the box they
disagree and the car responds to a given movement of the hands by 900/1080 of
what the game intends.
.Pp
.Sy "The easy way round is to set the game to 1080" ,
which costs one setting and needs nothing from
.Nm .
Measured on hardware: the tester did that and reported "the range is good,
it's similar to the normal T150 in Assetto Corsa on windows with the official
drivers".
.Pp
This option is the other direction, for driving at a shorter range than the
wheel's own: set the game to 540 and the wheel to 540 and they agree there
too.
.Sy "That direction has not been tested under a game" .
The wheel was at its own 1080 throughout the session that produced the result
above.
.Pp
Earlier versions of this page said the opposite, in bold: do not use this with
a game. That rested on a test which set 270, 900 and 1080 under a game and
felt no difference from any of them, run before the range had ever been
confirmed reaching the wheel. It measured a range that was not arriving, not
one that arrives and is unwelcome.
.Pp
.Xr t150ctl 1
sets the same thing by hand, which is the way to try a number before putting
it in a startup line.
.It Fl t
Re-send all three of an effect's packets whenever any one of them changes,
which is what builds before this one did. The default sends only the packet
that moved, which is a third of the writes and is what Thrustmaster's own
driver does: its DirectInput capture uploads an effect once and then
modulates it with bare update packets. Every write is a synchronous call on
the thread a game is waiting for, so two writes in three were costing a game
time to re-state bytes the wheel already had. This flag exists to compare the
two by feel on real hardware.
.It Fl w
Write to the wheel from a thread of its own. Without it every packet goes out
on the thread that also serves the socket, so while the daemon is inside a
synchronous
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
it cannot answer the proxy, and the game is blocked waiting for that answer on
its own main thread. That is measured: Assetto Corsa's warning does not appear
with
.Fl n ,
which runs everything except the writes.
.Pp
With
.Fl w
the writer owns the device outright. It acquires it, releases it, scans for it
and is the only thing that writes to it, so the file's long standing rule that
it is not reentrant is kept by nothing else ever coming in. The poll thread
copies bytes into a queue and returns.
.Pp
That answer is given before the write happens, so a write the wheel refuses
has nowhere to go: the frame that caused it was answered long ago. The writer
counts what it dropped instead, and the count only ever rises. Answering for
the packet in hand is the whole point of that: charging one packet's refusal
to the next made
.Fn flush_slot
stop a set of packets that had already gone, which puts a bare first packet on
the wire with no update or commit behind it.
.Pp
The session reads that count around its own writes rather than being told. A
watchdog safe state notes it before the stops and again after, and a rise
means the wheel is not where the daemon believes: it forgets what the wheel
holds, means to play the started slots again, and owes an error to the next
upload. So a refusal still keeps the slot owed and still re-arms the watchdog,
but the packet it is charged to is the one that failed. Without any of this a
refused packet was logged and dropped, and an effect could go missing, or a
stop fail to arrive, with every layer reporting success.
.Pp
A writer with no backlog can also let the emitter run ahead of its floor,
which is worth up to four milliseconds on a force the wheel could take
straight away. That is
.Fl E
and it is off. See
.Sx THE EMITTER .
.Pp
That queue coalesces, and it has to. The emitter flushes up to four dirty
slots, at worst every four milliseconds and sooner while this queue is empty,
and a constant costs two packets, so a game
holding three effects asks for at least 1250 packets a second, while the fastest
Thrustmaster's own driver ever puts two packets on that wire is 1.344
milliseconds apart, or about 740 a second. A queue that merely stored the
excess would spend the session full and deliver every force a fifth of a
second late, which is what the first build with a writer thread did. So a
packet still waiting when a newer one arrives for the same parameter is
replaced by it, in its place: the wheel holds one value per parameter, and the
one it never rendered was not going to be felt. A play and a stop for the same
slot are different parameters and never merge, and only the newest packet
waiting for a given target is merged into, so a repeat cannot overtake
something queued behind it: a stop, a play and a stop for one slot stay three
packets in that order.
.Pp
Under
.Fl v
the count of merged and refused packets is printed on the way out. Merged is
the queue working. Refused means 128 distinct packets were already waiting,
which is a wheel that has stopped taking writes rather than one that is merely
behind. A refusal is reported to the session as a failed write, so the effect
stays owed and the next pass sends it again; the queue never discards a packet
it has already accepted, because accepting one is what tells the session it
need not keep a copy.
.Pp
macOS only. The queue itself is checked by
.Pa tests/wirequeue_check ,
which needs no wheel, but the threading around it has only ever run on real
hardware.
.It Fl v
Log connections, effect downgrades, every entry into the safe state, and each
device setting as it is written, so a report can say whether the gain, the
range and the autocentre release reached the wheel rather than leaving all
three of sent, refused and never asked for looking the same.
Each connection is named by the port it came from, and whether its token was
accepted is logged too, so two connections can be told apart in a report.
.Pp
It also prints what the game asks for, at most once a second so a game
updating at its physics rate does not drown the terminal. A constant gives its
magnitude, direction and gain; a condition gives its centre, coefficients,
saturations and deadband, and is named as the
.Em spring
or
.Em damper
it is rather than as a condition, because the two are different forces and
this line could not tell them apart. The kind shown is the one the wheel was
given, after any downgrade, which is reported on its own line.
.Pp
A slot that starts or stops says so, once per transition rather than once per
call: a game may start an already playing slot on every frame, and Assetto
Corsa does, so a line per call would be several hundred a second. The question
this answers is whether a slot was ever started at all, because an effect that
is uploaded and never started renders nothing and looks from here exactly like
one the wheel ignores. Both lines are said after the write and say which way it
went, so neither can stand in a report as evidence for something that never
left the daemon: a start the wheel would not take says the write failed, and
the next one it does take says the slot started.
.Pp
A safe state names each playing slot it stopped, and says it after the writer's
queue has drained rather than when the packet was handed over, so a stop that
was only queued and then refused is not reported as one that landed. Without
those lines a slot silently lost its start in the middle of a session and the
only trace was the same slot being started twice with nothing between. A reset
says how many loaded effects it released and a stop-everything how many were
playing; both are silent when there was nothing to take, which is what keeps a
game that sends either of them often off the terminal.
.Pp
The watchdog carries the silence it measured, in milliseconds, because nothing
in this log is timestamped and that is the only duration in it: half a second
is a game that hitched and five seconds is a game or a socket that stopped, and
the two read the same without it.
.El
.Sh LOSING THE WHEEL
A wheel unplugged while a game is running takes the mach port behind its
device reference with it, and the next report fails with a status from below
IOKit rather than one of IOKit's own. Any status from outside IOKit's domain
is treated as the device having gone: the reference is released and the scan
for a wheel starts again.
.Pp
.Sy "A replug takes a few seconds to come back, and that is the wheel."
Four things happen in order, each on the daemon's half-second scan: it
notices the wheel has gone, finds it at the boot id, sends the switch, and
picks it up once it returns. Three of those are bounded by that scan and add
up to about a second and a half. The rest is the wheel: a switched T150
leaves the bus, enumerates again under its firmware id and runs its own
calibration sweep before it will answer anything, and nothing here can hurry
that. Scanning faster would shave a fraction off the smaller half and spend
CPU on the larger half doing nothing.
.Pp
That only covers a wheel somebody is still writing to. A replug is usually
not: the watchdog has fired, the session has dropped every effect, and a
keepalive writes nothing. With
.Fl w
the writer thread makes the same check on its own clock, because it owns the
device and the poll thread must not touch it; without that a replugged wheel
sat at the boot id until the daemon was restarted, which is the fault
.Fl w
reintroduced and this restores. So twice a second
.Nm
also asks the registry whether the wheel it believes it holds is still on the
bus, and lets go if it is not. Without that it kept a reference to a wheel
unplugged minutes earlier and never looked for its replacement, because the
thing that would have noticed was the write nobody was making.
.Pp
The wheel comes back at its boot product id, where every replug and every
wake leaves it, and
.Nm
opens only the firmware one. So a scan that finds nothing there looks for a
wheel at the boot id and switches it, and the scan after that finds it. The
switch is the same pair of endpoint 0 transfers
.Xr t150boot 8
sends, needs no privilege, and is refused for any model but the T150: another
wheel's switch value is not this one's, and an unattended caller is exactly
the one that must not guess. Somebody with another wheel runs
.Xr t150boot 8
with
.Fl V .
.Sh THE EMITTER
Effect parameters are state and are coalesced; everything else is an event
and is not.
.Pp
An
.Dv EFFECT_UPLOAD
records what the slot should hold and is answered at once. A pass then puts
it on the wheel, and a pass runs at most once every 4 milliseconds. A game
that updates a force faster than that has the superseded values dropped
rather than queued, because the wheel holds one value per slot and an
intermediate value was replaced before it could be felt. A game that
re-sends an effect it has not changed writes nothing at all: each slot keeps
the exact bytes it last put on the wire and a pass compares against them.
.Pp
Under
.Fl w
and
.Fl E
that floor is skipped while the writer has no backlog. A transfer already in
flight is not one: the pass this allows is the one that should be building the
next packet while the wheel takes that one. The floor was put there
when every write was a synchronous transfer on the thread the game was
waiting for, and pacing the daemon was the only thing keeping a frame out of
a burst of them; a writer thread paces itself, so the floor buys nothing
against an empty queue and costs up to a whole period on a force the wheel
could take now. It comes back the moment the queue has anything in it, which
is the case it was really for, and a pass that failed is never brought
forward, because the deadline is what stops a wheel that has gone turning the
retry into a spin. Without
.Fl w
nothing can answer the question and the floor always applies. Without
.Fl E
it applies anyway: see that option for why it is off.
.Pp
Only the packet that moved is sent. A level, a coefficient or a phase is one
packet; a change to the envelope, the duration or the effect's type is the
whole set, because those redefine the effect rather than move it. A bare
first or a bare commit is never sent.
.Pp
A constant force that is playing is then played again, which is the second
half of what the vendor does and is not optional. Sending the level alone was
tried on hardware and the force was reported as worse, which is what sent
this looking at the capture properly: 24 of its 25 bare updates are followed
immediately by a play. Its control panel captures for a spring and a sine
pair none, so nothing but the constant is re-played here.
.Pp
That is the vendor's own behaviour, taken from its DirectInput capture in
.Pa tmp/oldffb/directX_constforce.pcapng ,
which uploads an effect once and then sends twenty four bare update packets
as the force changes. This daemon used to re-send the set every time, on a
claim that the set was the only sequence measured, which was cited to a
research entry that says nothing of the kind. See
.Fl t .
.Pp
Starts, stops, destroys, resets, stop-all, the three settings, the input open
on hello, the watchdog, the safe state and shutdown are written the moment
they arrive and are never merged or deferred. A stop also drops whatever was
waiting for that slot, so no parameter packet follows an effect that has just
been stopped.
.Pp
Because the write happens after the frame that caused it has been answered, a
device error is reported on the next
.Dv EFFECT_UPLOAD
and on nothing else. It is not reported on a keepalive: the proxy drops its
connection on any error reply and nothing reconnects, so one absent wheel
would cost a game its force feedback for the life of the process.
.Pp
Re-acquiring the wheel scrubs every slot, so the backend says when that has
happened and the next pass teaches the wheel every effect again, and then
starts the ones the game had asked to play.
.Pp
Replaying the start is deliberate and was once deliberately not done, on the
reasoning that a wheel which begins pushing by itself after a replug is not
an improvement. That was wrong in the case that happens. A game is not told
its effect stopped, because from its side nothing failed, so it never starts
it again; the choice is not between a quiet wheel and a pushing one but
between force feedback that returns and force feedback that is gone until the
game is restarted. The start a game asked for is remembered even when the
wheel was already off the bus and the packet could not be sent.
.Sh DEVICE SETTINGS
The wheel keeps a device gain and a rotation range of its own, which survive
the process that set them. The gain scales every force it renders.
.Nm
sets it to full when a client says hello, because a wheel that has been away
has forgotten it. Full means do not attenuate: it is DirectInput's own default
for
.Dv DIPROP_FFGAIN ,
so the strength a driver feels is the one the game's settings ask for. A game
that wants less says so and
.Xr t150ctl 1
overrides it by hand.
.Pp
After that it is the gain the client last asked for that is restored, not full
scale, whenever the wheel is re-acquired. The proxy sends
.Dv DIPROP_FFGAIN
once, when the game sets it, and has no path to send it again, so writing full
here used to be the end of the driver's chosen strength: a wheel replugged mid
race, or a daemon restarted under a running game, put every force back to full
with nothing anywhere reporting it.
.Pp
The rotation range is left alone unless
.Fl r
gave one, and is then applied at the same two moments. A client that sets one
over the protocol replaces it for the rest of its session, and that is what a
re-acquire puts back, on the same rule as the gain. No game does: DirectInput
has no property for a rotation range, which is why
.Fl r
exists.
.Sh THE ENDPOINT FILE
.Nm
binds an ephemeral port and writes two lines, the port and a per-run token:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
49713
6a607d5fcea96d0e8c3356cc56790fe9
.Ed
.Pp
It is written to a temporary and renamed, so a client never reads half a
file, and created with mode 0600. The token stops another local process
driving the motors by guessing a port. It is not a security boundary against
a process running as the same user, and it is not meant to be: what bounds
the damage is the watchdog.
.Sh THE WATCHDOG
Nothing in this stack learns that a game has exited. Wine's
.Pa hidclass.sys
consumes the close at the physical device object, and DirectInput sends a
reset only on a graceful unacquire, so a game that crashes or is force quit
leaves the wheel holding whatever force it was last given.
.Pp
.Nm
therefore treats silence as a fault. If no frame arrives for half a second it
stops every effect and puts the autocenter back to
.Fl a ,
whether or not the socket is still open, which leaves the wheel limp rather
than latched unless a spring was asked for. A client is
expected to send
.Dv T150_OP_KEEPALIVE
often enough to stay ahead of this. The same safe state is entered when a
client disconnects, when one displaces another, and on the way out.
.Pp
.Sy "It protects against a game that has gone, not one that has hung" .
The proxy's keepalive runs on a timer of its own and knows nothing about
whether the game is still running its frame loop, so a game whose logic thread
deadlocks keeps the watchdog fed for as long as its process is scheduled, and
whatever force it had commanded stays on the wheel. That is a trade and not an
oversight: the keepalive exists because a game holding one steady force and
calling nothing otherwise lost it after half a second, and at this layer a
game holding a force deliberately and a game hung while holding one are the
same silence. Killing a hung game is still something a person has to do.
.Pp
A stop the wheel will not take is owed rather than forgotten. The slot keeps
its record, the daemon stays armed, and the stop is tried again on the
emitter's cadence and on every watchdog wake until the wheel accepts it. What
does go down immediately is the game's intent, so an effect the game stopped is
never started again by the replay that follows a re-acquire, however the stop
itself fared.
The debt also survives everything that replaces what the daemon knows about a
slot without changing what the wheel is rendering: the game uploading a new
effect to that slot, a second client displacing the first, and a client simply
going away and another connecting. Each of those used to take the record with
them and leave the wheel pulling with nothing able to stop it. The last one is
the quietest, because between the disconnect and the reconnect the emitter is
retrying the stop on every pass, and it is the arrival of the next game that
ends the retry.
.Pp
When the stop does land, the slot is left exactly as one the wheel took at once
would have been. A plain
.Dv T150_OP_EFFECT_STOP
leaves the effect downloaded and startable again, so a refused write is not the
difference between stopping an effect and destroying it; a slot holding no
effect at all, which is what a debt inherited from a displaced client is, goes
with the debt that was its only reason to exist.
.Pp
A connection that never proved the token is the exception. Its input was
never opened and it sent nothing to render, so there is nothing of its doing
to undo, and it leaves without the wheel being written to at all. Anything on
the machine can open the port, and connecting to it must not reach the
hardware. The release on the way out is unconditional, because the wheel's
input is also opened and closed on
.Nm Ns 's
own account.
.Sh EFFECTS
The wheel renders constant force, square, triangle, sine, both sawtooths,
spring and damper. Friction, inertia and ramp are not in its protocol.
.Nm
downgrades those rather than refusing them, because a game that gets
.Dv DIERR_UNSUPPORTED
from
.Fn CreateEffect
may switch force feedback off altogether: friction and inertia become damper,
and a ramp becomes a constant whose level
.Nm
re-computes every 20 milliseconds as it slides. Whether a recomputed level is
worth a packet is the emitter's question, so a slide that does not move the
level far enough to change a byte on the wire sends nothing.
.Pp
A per-effect gain has no equivalent on the wheel, which has one device gain,
so it is folded into the effect's magnitude before encoding.
.Pp
.Sy "A condition's coefficient is held at 80 percent of what the wheel takes" .
This is the one place
.Nm
knowingly gives a game less than it asked for, and it is a workaround for the
wheel rather than a correction of the game. The T150's own condition loop is
unstable at the top of its range: measured by hand with no game, no daemon and
no proxy, a damper at 100 makes the wheel buzz wherever it is left standing,
99 still buzzes, 90 buzzes more slowly, and 80 is quiet. An oscillation that
slows as the gain falls is the wheel fighting itself.
.Pp
The encoding is not what is wrong. Thrustmaster's own divisor takes the same
request to 100 as well, so the wheel dislikes a value that is faithfully
encoded. What justifies overriding a game here is that the value cannot be
reached from the game's own settings: Assetto Corsa asks for 9998 of 10000 and
its force feedback page has no damping control at all, so nobody can work
around this themselves. The coefficient is clamped rather than rescaled, so a
game asking for half strength still gets half strength and only the top of the
range is flattened. See
.Pa docs/RESEARCH.md
A46.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/Library/Application Support/t150ffb/endpoint
The port and token, removed on exit.
.It Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents/it.allard.t150d.plist
The login agent the application writes when
.Em Start at login
is switched on. It names this daemon inside the application's own bundle,
carries the rotation and the spring on its command line, has
.Li KeepAlive
so a daemon that dies is replaced, and sends both output streams to the log
below.
.It Pa ~/Library/LaunchAgents/it.allard.t150menu.plist
The other half of the same switch, which starts the menu bar application. It
has no
.Li KeepAlive ,
because an application somebody quits has to stay quit.
