.Sh CAVEATS
The wheel honours settings and force feedback alike through an unprivileged
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
with the device left to macOS, which is the transport this daemon uses. Both
were measured; the procedure is in
.Pa docs/PROBES.md .
.Pp
This daemon has driven a wheel end to end, first from
.Xr t150-probe 1 Ns 's
in-bottle probe and then from a game: Assetto Corsa creates its effects
through the proxy and the encode and slot path renders them on a T150, with
the tester reporting the result as similar to the wheel's own Windows drivers.
RESEARCH.md A43.
.Pp
Springs, envelopes, ramps and per-effect gain are arithmetic derived from
a Linux driver, and now checked against Thrustmaster's own Windows driver
field by field, so the bytes are right even though nobody has felt those
effects on a wheel.
.Pp
The emitter's single packet path has been felt: sending only the packet that
moved, rather than all three, is what the wheel is given by the driver that
works, and the tester noticed the difference when the update went out on its
own without the play that follows it. What no measurement has settled is the
rate. Whether a wheel notices the difference between an update every 3
milliseconds and one every 4, and whether it prefers the reduced traffic, are
questions only hardware answers, and
.Fl t
exists to compare the two by feel.
